1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,261
CAROL:

2
00:00:00,261 --> 00:00:02,463
It’s really good to see you, Tom.

3
00:00:02,463 --> 00:00:06,843
Since I had to give up work, I feel I’m losing touch with my friends

4
00:00:06,843 --> 00:00:07,390
and colleagues.

5
00:00:07,390 --> 00:00:07,440
TOM:

6
00:00:07,440 --> 00:00:10,360
We really miss you in the office – we were all

7
00:00:10,360 --> 00:00:11,980
so sorry you had to leave.

8
00:00:11,980 --> 00:00:15,760
But you must be relieved to have more time to look after your mother.

9
00:00:15,760 --> 00:00:16,570
How is she?

10
00:00:16,570 --> 00:00:16,888
CAROL:

11
00:00:16,888 --> 00:00:20,700
Well, she’s very cheerful but she needs a lot of help.

12
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:20,946
TOM:

13
00:00:20,946 --> 00:00:23,650
Have you tried to get any support from the local council?

14
00:00:23,650 --> 00:00:23,976
CAROL:

15
00:00:23,976 --> 00:00:24,302
No.

16
00:00:24,302 --> 00:00:26,260
I didn’t know I could.

17
00:00:26,260 --> 00:00:26,596
TOM:

18
00:00:26,596 --> 00:00:29,285
Yes, they offer different kinds of practical support.

19
00:00:29,285 --> 00:00:33,992
They realise that carers sometimes need time for all the other responsibilities they have

20
00:00:33,992 --> 00:00:36,681
apart from the person they’re caring for,

21
00:00:36,681 --> 00:00:39,370
and also that they sometimes need a break.

22
00:00:39,370 --> 00:00:39,917
CAROL:

23
00:00:39,917 --> 00:00:40,465
Absolutely.

24
00:00:40,465 --> 00:00:41,012
OK,

25
00:00:41,012 --> 00:00:43,201
so tell me more.

26
00:00:43,201 --> 00:00:47,580
How do I go about getting this support?

27
00:00:47,580 --> 00:00:47,810
TOM:

28
00:00:47,810 --> 00:00:50,800
Well, you’d have to have an assessment of your mother’s needs.

29
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:51,055
TOM:

30
00:00:51,055 --> 00:00:54,370
That means someone would come round and talk to you about the situation

31
00:00:54,370 --> 00:00:55,390
and what you need.

32
00:00:55,390 --> 00:00:55,604
TOM:

33
00:00:55,604 --> 00:00:58,815
So for a start they’d want to know the amount of time you spend

34
00:00:58,815 --> 00:01:00,100
looking after your mother every day.

35
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:00,625
CAROL:

36
00:01:00,625 --> 00:01:01,150
OK.

37
00:01:01,150 --> 00:01:01,413
TOM:

38
00:01:01,413 --> 00:01:05,353
Then they’ll probably ask you what sorts of tasks you do for your mother

39
00:01:05,353 --> 00:01:06,141
during the day.

40
00:01:06,141 --> 00:01:09,030
Things like if she needs help with getting dressed, for example.

41
00:01:09,030 --> 00:01:09,335
CAROL:

42
00:01:09,335 --> 00:01:09,641
Right.

43
00:01:09,641 --> 00:01:11,167
I help her with that,

44
00:01:11,167 --> 00:01:14,830
and also I help her get into the shower in the morning.

45
00:01:14,830 --> 00:01:15,137
TOM:

46
00:01:15,137 --> 00:01:15,445
Yes,

47
00:01:15,445 --> 00:01:16,674
that sort of thing.

48
00:01:16,674 --> 00:01:20,362
They’ll probably ask you if you do the shopping for her,

49
00:01:20,362 --> 00:01:21,899
and help her at mealtimes.

50
00:01:21,899 --> 00:01:24,050
And whether she can cope using money.

51
00:01:24,050 --> 00:01:24,386
CAROL:

52
00:01:24,386 --> 00:01:24,723
Yes,

53
00:01:24,723 --> 00:01:27,414
that’s becoming a bit of a problem.

54
00:01:27,414 --> 00:01:30,105
She used to be very good at it,

55
00:01:30,105 --> 00:01:31,450
but not any more.

56
00:01:31,450 --> 00:01:31,772
TOM:

57
00:01:31,772 --> 00:01:36,275
And be ready to tell them about anything you find particularly difficult about caring

58
00:01:36,275 --> 00:01:37,240
for your mother.

59
00:01:37,240 --> 00:01:37,579
CAROL:

60
00:01:37,579 --> 00:01:43,000
So recently I’ve noticed she’s started to have quite bad problems with her memory

61
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:43,050
...

62
00:01:43,050 --> 00:01:47,744
if I wasn’t there, I think she’d forget to eat, for example.

63
00:01:47,744 --> 00:01:51,810
And often she doesn’t seem quite sure what day it is.

64
00:01:51,810 --> 00:01:52,186
TOM:

65
00:01:52,186 --> 00:01:54,064
Yes, tell them about that.

66
00:01:54,064 --> 00:01:58,197
And are there any physical difficulties you have caring for her?

67
00:01:58,197 --> 00:01:59,700
Lifting her, for example?

68
00:01:59,700 --> 00:02:00,057
CAROL:

69
00:02:00,057 --> 00:02:00,413
Yes.

70
00:02:00,413 --> 00:02:01,839
She’s quite heavy,

71
00:02:01,839 --> 00:02:07,187
and I’m afraid of hurting my back – I’d be in real trouble if

72
00:02:07,187 --> 00:02:07,900
that happened.

73
00:02:07,900 --> 00:02:08,165
TOM:

74
00:02:08,165 --> 00:02:10,020
They can give you advice about that,

75
00:02:10,020 --> 00:02:13,730
and also about how to avoid the possibility of your mum having a fall.

76
00:02:13,730 --> 00:02:14,228
CAROL:

77
00:02:14,228 --> 00:02:14,725
Great.

78
00:02:14,725 --> 00:02:21,690
So once they’ve done this assessment, if I’m eligible, what happens next?

79
00:02:21,690 --> 00:02:21,979
TOM:

80
00:02:21,979 --> 00:02:23,716
Well, they might support you financially.

81
00:02:23,716 --> 00:02:27,767
So they might help you with transport costs, like if you have to get

82
00:02:27,767 --> 00:02:30,950
a taxi to take your mother for an appointment, for example.

83
00:02:30,950 --> 00:02:31,356
CAROL:

84
00:02:31,356 --> 00:02:33,795
I usually drive her myself actually.

85
00:02:33,795 --> 00:02:36,640
So could I claim for the petrol?

86
00:02:36,640 --> 00:02:36,915
TOM:

87
00:02:36,915 --> 00:02:37,464
You could,

88
00:02:37,464 --> 00:02:39,660
and you can claim for the insurance too.

89
00:02:39,660 --> 00:02:40,090
CAROL:

90
00:02:40,090 --> 00:02:40,950
Oh, right.

91
00:02:40,950 --> 00:02:41,199
TOM:

92
00:02:41,199 --> 00:02:45,437
And if you need help with the housework, they can arrange for someone to come along once

93
00:02:45,437 --> 00:02:46,434
or twice a week.

94
00:02:46,434 --> 00:02:49,675
And one other thing, I hope you don’t mind me saying this,

95
00:02:49,675 --> 00:02:51,669
but it’s important you look after yourself,

96
00:02:51,669 --> 00:02:54,910
and it seems to me you’re under quite a bit of stress.

97
00:02:54,910 --> 00:02:55,338
CAROL:

98
00:02:55,338 --> 00:02:56,620
I am, yes.

99
00:02:56,620 --> 00:02:56,901
TOM:

100
00:02:56,901 --> 00:02:58,025
Well, tell the council,

101
00:02:58,025 --> 00:03:01,959
because they may be able to give you some advice on how to minimise

102
00:03:01,959 --> 00:03:02,240
it.

103
00:03:02,240 --> 00:03:02,738
CAROL:

104
00:03:02,738 --> 00:03:03,237
Really?

105
00:03:03,237 --> 00:03:08,720
Though actually I feel so much better having talked to you.

106
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:13,705
I’ll get in touch with the council straight away.

107
00:03:13,705 --> 00:03:15,200
Now shall we ...
